  "answer": "Some people need to use a dialysis machine when their kidneys are damaged and can no longer perform their function of removing waste and excess fluids from the blood. This can happen due to various reasons such as infections, injury, high blood pressure, high blood sugar, or restricted blood flow to the kidneys. When the kidneys fail, poisonous wastes accumulate in the body, which can lead to serious health issues or even death. A dialysis machine helps filter the blood in a process called 'haemodialysis'. In this process, blood is taken out from the main artery, mixed with an anticoagulant like heparin, and then pumped into a dialyzer. The dialyzer contains tubes made of cellophane, which are surrounded by dialyzing fluid. This fluid has the same composition as plasma but without nitrogenous wastes. The nitrogenous wastes from the blood move out into the dialyzing fluid through a thin membrane, thereby cleaning the blood. The cleaned blood is then returned to the body through a vein. This process mimics the function of the kidney but does not involve reabsorption. Each dialysis session typically lasts for 3 to 6 hours and is used by thousands of patients worldwide who suffer from kidney failure.",
